1
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,320
Are you kidding me?

2
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,280
Sir, we need to be moving out.

3
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,320
- The plane is on standby. - I say when the plane leaves, Kevin.

4
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:08,600
What do you need, money?

5
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:13,120
It's not the money, sir. It's the logistics and the scale.

6
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:15,640
It's too big, too complex.

7
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,600
And, of course, the confidentiality, it's potentially very exposing

8
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:20,920
for all of us.

9
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,040
But especially for you.

10
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:25,520
Frankie,

11
00:01:27,120 --> 00:01:28,400
we're family, right?

12
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:30,800
I mean, you're my...

13
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:35,880
- What are you again? - I'm your niece's wife, sir.

14
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,200
Exactly. But you get things done.

15
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:41,280
Now, this could destroy me for 2020.

16
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,480
So make it disappear.

17
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:47,120
I'm not sure I can.

18
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:51,840
What?

19
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:53,240
Hi.

20
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:55,800
Thought I was the only one in.

21
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:00,240
I just came in to check everything was all right before we opened.

22
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:01,480
Who the hell is this?

23
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:06,800
I'm Najia. I... I didn't realise that you were in here

24
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,200
or here at all. Wow.

25
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,000
I thought I told you to stay on the door, Kevin.

26
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,560
I came through the door to tell you we had to leave, sir.

27
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,080
Unbelievable.

28
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:17,720
What, do you, uh, work for me or something?

29
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,640
Yes, I'll be the general manager.

30
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:21,400
Er, I thought while we were finalising

31
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:24,080
I'd come in and familiarise myself so I can hit the ground running

32
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:26,000
- when we officially open. - You're fired.

33
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:27,680
Pardon?

34
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:30,240
You're fired. Get out.

35
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,320
- I'm so sorry. - You have one hour

36
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,560
to tell me how you're gonna make this all go away.

37
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:37,800
Okay?

38
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:41,000
Kevin, I say when we go.

39
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:01,720
We're nearly there.

40
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:06,840
Are you sure you've got this under control?

41
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:09,200
Totally. New systems.

42
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:11,680
Just running them in.

43
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:33,720
We're home.

44
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:36,000
We're actually home!

45
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:40,400
Yes! Result. See? I told you I'd do it.

46
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:42,320
We're at Park Hill.

47
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:45,480
- That's my flat, there. - What, you live at Park Hill?

48
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:46,600
We're just up there.

49
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,480
And I've got a mobile signal again.

50
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,240
But no messages.

51
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:53,760
But, to be fair, it's only half an hour since you left.

52
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:56,720
What, half an hour since we were with you in that warehouse?

53
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:57,880
Yeah.

54
00:03:58,520 --> 00:03:59,640
Wow.

55
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:01,800
So...

56
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:05,920
I suppose this is it.

57
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:10,160
Yeah, I suppose it is.

58
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:12,920
Got you back. Guess we're done.

59
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,120
Nice having you aboard.

60
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:17,240
Thanks, Doc.

61
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:19,080
It's been a blast, truly.

62
00:04:20,040 --> 00:04:21,280
Pleasure.

63
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,320
What are you gonna do now?

64
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:27,840
Oh, you know, back in the box.

65
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:30,840
- There's loads to see. - By yourself?

66
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:34,760
Yeah, I suppose.

67
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,640
- Do you want to come for tea at mine? - Definitely!

68
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,440
Yes, I would. Thanks. I love tea.

69
00:04:43,640 --> 00:04:45,280
Tea at Yaz's? Amazing!

70
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,040
Are you coming? Are we all going for tea at Yaz's?

71
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:50,000
She didn't invite us.

72
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:52,800
Don't be daft. Of course you're invited.

73
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,120
All right, then.

74
00:04:56,720 --> 00:04:57,720
Graham?

75
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:01,560
I think I'll nip home first, Yaz.

76
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:03,880
Maybe join you later, if that's all right.

77
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,160
Shall I come with you?

78
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,200
I'd rather do it alone.

79
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:17,680
Don't go without saying goodbye.

80
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,680
Tea at Yaz's. Never been for tea at Yaz's!

81
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:29,480
I can't believe you live here.

82
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:30,880
Anna!

83
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,840
I can see this block from my window.

84
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:36,720
Almost neighbours all this time.

85
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:38,600
I know.

86
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:40,640
- It's cool. - Yeah.

87
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:45,000
- Everything all right? - Fine, thanks.

88
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:48,440
Anna, it'sJade Mclntyre.

89
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:50,640
I'm outside your front door. Are you in?

90
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:53,480
I'm home. Got a couple of mates with me.

91
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:56,200
Probably all out.

92
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:57,560
You brought friends back?

93
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,800
Sonya, Yaz has brought friends back!

94
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:02,040
I'm getting food.

95
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,200
What, you actually have friends?

96
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:06,680
Is she paying you?

97
00:06:09,320 --> 00:06:10,960
Oh, look at your views!

98
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:12,600
Never had a flat.

99
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,440
I should get one. I'd be good in a flat.

100
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:17,040
I could get a sofa. Imagine me with a sofa.

101
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:20,800
Like, my own sofa. I could get a... A purple one, and sit on it.

102
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,760
- Am I being weird? - A little bit, yeah.

103
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:26,520
I'm trying to do small talk. I thought I was doing quite well.

104
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:27,800
Needs work.

105
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:29,600
Maybe I'm nervous.

106
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:32,560
Or just socially awkward. I'm still figuring myself out.

107
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:39,080
You really like junk. Are you collecting it, like stamps?

108
00:06:39,160 --> 00:06:42,280
- Ah, let me tell you about this mess. - Don't get him started.

109
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:44,440
Dad, we keep telling you, stop picking it up.

110
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:47,120
Mum's gonna go crazy when she sees you've brought it home again.

111
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:48,400
It stinks!

112
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:51,200
- I can't just leave it there. - Why do we have to have it?

113
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:52,320
Well, I thought, maybe

114
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,320
- as your sister is a policewoman... - Police officer.

115
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,440
And she said she'd do something about it.

116
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,000
I did, and they're looking into it.

117
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,240
Well, not fast enough. It's a disgrace.

118
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:02,480
Don't keep it in the kitchen. Put it down the chute.

119
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:05,800
It's evidence, and you know what it's evidence of.

120
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,120
A conspiracy.

121
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:09,320
Exactly, a total conspiracy!

122
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:10,680
And getting worse.

123
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:12,600
I love a conspiracy.

124
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:17,080
- So, are you and Yaz... - Mates.

125
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:19,520
We were at primary school together.

126
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Just mates, then?

127
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:22,360
Yeah.

128
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:24,040
That's good.

129
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:28,320
- Hi, Mum? - Hey.

130
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,360
- Can you pick me up from work? - What?

131
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:33,120
- Now? Er, I'm just in the middle of... - Yes. Now.

132
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:34,760
It's urgent.

133
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,240
I'll text you the address.

134
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:39,160
Don't let on to your dad.

135
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:40,880
I'll tell you everything when I see you.

136
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:42,920
- Fine. - Thanks, sweetheart.

137
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:50,040
Is she all right?

138
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,560
Yeah, she forgot something.

139
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,600
Asked if I'd drop it at work for her.

140
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,920
She's got a new job, posh hotel opening.

141
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:00,960
Er, do you mind if I nip out quickly?

142
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:03,600
- Need any company? - Nah. I'll be fine.

143
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,480
Er, we'll look after your friends.

144
00:08:05,600 --> 00:08:08,040
- I'm gonna make pakora. - Dad, don't.

145
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:11,120
He's terrible at pakora.

146
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:13,760
We never meet your friends! She never brings anyone around.

147
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:14,800
Married to the job.

148
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:16,800
At least I've got a job to be married to.

149
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:18,600
- Bye. - Hope you don't crash.

150
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:19,880
Girls, now.

151
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,400
Sisters.

152
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:23,960
I used to have sisters. I used to be a sister

153
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:25,360
in an aqua hospital.

154
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:29,440
It actually turned out to be a training camp for the Quiston Calcium Assassins.

155
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:30,720
Going off on one again.

156
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:33,120
Ooh, you've got a parcel to pick up, left with a neighbour.

157
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:35,160
Yeah, I've been trying to get that for days.

158
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:37,120
A couple of doors down. No reply.

159
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:39,720
Do you want me to go get it while you make your terrible pakora?

160
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:12,560
You'll have to learn how to change the hoover bag now.

161
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:16,440
And work out where I kept the spares.

162
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,640
Rubbish collection is every Friday.

163
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:22,600
Recycling, every other week.

164
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:24,680
I know.

165
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,040
I know.

166
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:31,640
I've got so much to tell you.

167
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:43,360
No word from Graham?

168
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:45,000
Do you think I should have gone with him?

169
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:46,880
- You know him better than me. - Not much.

170
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:48,480
Still no answer?

171
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:50,760
We've got a package to pick up for number 34.

172
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:52,640
Think she took it in. Are you a friend?

173
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:56,400
We work together at the uni. She hasn't been in for a few days.

174
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:59,080
Didn't call in sick, isn't answering her messages.

175
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:01,640
Thought I'd drop by, see if she's okay.

176
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:04,360
- I'm Jade. - I'm Ryan.

177
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:07,240
- That's the Doctor. - Hello, Anna?

178
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:09,960
It's next door but one. Have you got a parcel for us?

179
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:12,440
Mmm.

180
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,040
I mean,

181
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:16,840
- I could open the door. - Mmm.

182
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:18,480
What, like, break it in?

183
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,600
No, just sort the lock.

184
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:22,320
If you thought it was appropriate.

185
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:25,080
If you're worried about her.

186
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:26,600
I am.

187
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:31,960
Anna?

188
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:37,000
How long did you say it'd been since you'd seen her?

189
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:41,560
- Power's out. - A few days.

190
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:45,360
A lot of cobwebs for a few days.

191
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:48,080
Yeah, that's what I was thinking.

192
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:49,160
Oh.

193
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:53,320
- Whoa. This is proper weird, now. - Yeah.

194
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:04,160
Graham O'Brien,

195
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:07,880
what are you doing sniffing coats?

196
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:12,720
How's that going to help, eh?

197
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:20,600
Did Anna ever mention she had a problem with spiders?

198
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:34,480
Let's take a look downstairs.

199
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:40,480
What is that?

200
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:43,600
Anna?

201
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:44,840
Is she in?

202
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,080
You okay?

203
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:55,800
Anna?

204
00:11:58,760 --> 00:11:59,880
Oh my days!

205
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:02,080
My God! Anna!

206
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:03,280
I'm sorry.

207
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:05,800
Spiders don't do that.

208
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:08,040
Do they?

209
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:09,480
Wrong question, Ryan.

210
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,320
You should be asking, "Where's the spider that did this?"

211
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:13,640
You think it's still in here?

212
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:16,320
Maybe.

213
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:26,320
Three,

214
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:27,880
two...

215
00:12:41,520 --> 00:12:43,200
God!

216
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:46,480
Look at the size of it!

217
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:49,560
No sudden moves.

218
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,560
It's domestic, but it's way too big.

219
00:12:53,680 --> 00:12:55,640
- It's not harmful. - It suffocated your mate.

220
00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:57,360
Let's put a door between us.

221
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:59,480
When I say "now," quick as you can.

222
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:02,520
Go, go, go!

223
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:03,760
Go!

224
00:13:04,600 --> 00:13:07,120
Ryan, keep it in there. Back in a sec.

225
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,800
We're out. It's contained.

226
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:11,520
Only a spider, a big spider, but only a spider.

227
00:13:11,600 --> 00:13:13,280
Not mad keen on spiders.

228
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,240
- Erm... - What?

229
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:20,840
Doctor, it's coming through!

230
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:23,520
We can't keep it in the bedroom.

231
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:27,760
Rubbish chute. Maybe that's how it got in.

232
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:29,080
Could we drive it out through there?

233
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:30,840
And set it loose across the city? No.

234
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:33,920
We need to keep it isolated here.

235
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:35,760
Ryan, get up here.

236
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,440
Oi, you just covered me in vinegar.

237
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:42,920
Spiders' feet are their noses and they hate the smell of garlic.

238
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:45,720
And the acetic acid in the vinegar means it won't come any further.

239
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:55,360
Where is it?

240
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:12,800
Hi. We don't mean you any harm.

241
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:14,360
You're not supposed to be this big,

242
00:14:14,440 --> 00:14:16,480
and you're definitely not supposed to attack humans.

243
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:19,920
You stay here until I figure this out.

244
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:22,160
Deal?

245
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:30,240
Let's go.

246
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:32,320
Ugh.

247
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:34,520
Oh, I did not like that.

248
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:39,120
Jade Mclntyre, who are you, exactly?

249
00:14:39,240 --> 00:14:42,240
I saw you check those cobwebs and you weren't surprised.

250
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:44,160
Hey!

251
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:46,520
You'll never guess what I just found in the loft room.

252
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:48,760
- It better not be a massive spider. - Yeah.

253
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:50,680
Like a big spider, just shed its skin.

254
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:53,600
A woman has died, and I think you know more than you're telling.

255
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:57,440
This isn't the first incident.

256
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:00,760
Something's happening with the spiders in this city.

257
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:02,560
They're out of control.

258
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:10,520
Long time to get a parcel.

259
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:12,760
She always has the weirdest friends.

260
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:22,640
The place where Anna worked with me, it's just over the way.

261
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:24,760
I'm a research fellow in zoology,

262
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:26,560
specialising in arachnids and arthropods,

263
00:15:26,760 --> 00:15:29,480
but spiders are our main focus,

264
00:15:29,560 --> 00:15:32,480
and we're seeing something very wrong in their behaviour right now.

265
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:38,280
- How's the house? - Yeah, fine, yeah.

266
00:15:38,360 --> 00:15:40,760
- Good to be home, eh? - Yeah.

267
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:43,760
Look, I found this on the mat.

268
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:47,000
That's your dad's handwriting, innit?

269
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:48,760
Looks like it, yeah.

270
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:51,760
- Well, you're not going to open it? - No.

271
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:11,560
This is my insurance policy.

272
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,240
Everything I did was under strict instruction,

273
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:18,960
and non-disclosure agreements, and against my better judgment.

274
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:22,000
And I hope one day I can forgive myself for my part in it.

275
00:16:26,920 --> 00:16:28,040
What's that?

276
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:31,280
Oh, my God!

277
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:43,800
Flippin' heck, Mum.

278
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:50,640
Quick, get in before anyone sees.

279
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:53,360
- Hi. - Oh!

280
00:16:53,560 --> 00:16:57,040
What's the big hug for? I only saw you this morning!

281
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:00,280
Yeah, well, it feels longer.

282
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:02,960
This is well swanky.

283
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:06,400
Don't get attached. We need to go.

284
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:08,480
Can I not have a quick look around?

285
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:09,560
No, Yaz.

286
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:12,960
- I've been sacked. - What?

287
00:17:13,360 --> 00:17:15,800
Mum... But you've just started.

288
00:17:16,120 --> 00:17:19,360
- I know. - They can't do that. What happened?

289
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:21,160
- Who sacked you? - I did.

290
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,720
Hands in the air, both of you.

291
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:26,440
As of right now, you're both trespassing upon my property.

292
00:17:26,520 --> 00:17:29,160
Put the gun down, please, sir.

293
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:33,320
My hotel, my rules. You were fired.

294
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:36,360
Yet, instead of leaving, you bring someone else in.

295
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:38,760
She's my daughter and she's giving me a lift home.

296
00:17:39,160 --> 00:17:42,400
- Oh, how very moving. - Why'd you fire her?

297
00:17:42,480 --> 00:17:43,880
I don't answer to you.

298
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:46,320
What are you, 15? You should be in school!

299
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:49,960
Your mother isn't supposed to be here. I told the staff to take two days off.

300
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:52,760
I came in to do extra preparation before we opened.

301
00:17:52,840 --> 00:17:55,120
Well, I really don't care,

302
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:58,000
because if I hadn't fired you then, I would have fired you 10 minutes ago

303
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:00,000
when I saw the rooms. Disgraceful!

304
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:02,360
What rooms? I don't understand.

305
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:03,840
I'm sure you don't understand.

306
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:06,720
Would you like to see how good your mother really isn't?

307
00:18:08,720 --> 00:18:10,920
We reckon there could be around 21 quadrillion spiders

308
00:18:10,960 --> 00:18:12,120
on the planet in total.

309
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:14,000
So what sort of research are you doing in here?

310
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:17,040
We're interested in utilising the genetic strengths of arachnids.

311
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:20,520
Ordinary spider silk is as strong as steel, or as tough as Kevlar.

312
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:21,960
Still don't make me like 'em.

313
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:23,120
Ooh, fun fact.

314
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:25,840
If you weave dragline spider silk as thick as a pencil,

315
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:27,560
it's strong enough to stop a plane in flight.

316
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:29,120
- Mmm? - You're kidding!

317
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:31,440
I'm not. I've had to deal with it.

318
00:18:31,720 --> 00:18:33,840
Well, me and Amelia Earheart.

319
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:35,640
You'd like her. She's a right laugh.

320
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:38,640
So, what else are you doing in here?

321
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:42,080
I've been working on an enzyme to increase their lifespan.

322
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:45,080
Spiders can keep growing for as long as they live.

323
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:47,960
That spider in Anna's flat was way bigger than a normal household spider.

324
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,000
- Yeah. - Did Anna

325
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:51,200
- have access to your experiments? - No!

326
00:18:51,440 --> 00:18:52,960
She was on the admin team.

327
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:54,760
Everything we do here is secure.

328
00:18:55,160 --> 00:18:58,520
We discard all carcasses responsibly through a specialist company.

329
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,680
Unless she was taking things without us knowing,

330
00:19:02,520 --> 00:19:03,920
but she's not that kind of person.

331
00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:06,680
I should notify the police.

332
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:09,480
What is it you wanted to show us?

333
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:13,360
Reports of unusual spider activity in Sheffield over the last three months.

334
00:19:13,760 --> 00:19:17,680
From the police, pest controllers and to us, here.

335
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:20,480
Rare sightings, an increase in numbers,

336
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:22,520
spiders you don't normally see at this time of year.

337
00:19:22,560 --> 00:19:26,000
Something's wrong with the spider ecosystem in South Yorkshire.

338
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:28,760
Exactly, but we don't know what.

339
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,200
They have nothing in common.

340
00:19:30,240 --> 00:19:32,480
Different species, different quantities.

341
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:36,600
Some are large massings, some have been a profusion of web-building.

342
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:41,640
I can't work out if they're confused, angry, or scared.

343
00:19:42,440 --> 00:19:44,080
Or trying to send a message.

344
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:50,120
What are you doing?

345
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:58,840
Where's that?

346
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:02,800
Now, if you're so great,

347
00:20:02,880 --> 00:20:04,640
explain this.

348
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:06,160
Hmm?

349
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:08,640
I can't... That's not possible.

350
00:20:08,720 --> 00:20:10,320
You're fired. Again.

351
00:20:12,560 --> 00:20:15,440
Oh, it's time for my scheduled bathroom break.

352
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:17,600
Kevin, we'll go next door.

353
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:22,480
And, when I'm done, Kevin will escort you off the grounds.

354
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:23,560
Forever.

355
00:20:25,160 --> 00:20:26,920
Scheduled bathroom breaks?

356
00:20:27,120 --> 00:20:29,080
I checked this room yesterday,

357
00:20:29,160 --> 00:20:30,360
it wasn't like this.

358
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:36,160
Did you hear that?

359
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:38,160
Yeah.

360
00:21:07,680 --> 00:21:09,280
Hello?

361
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:13,280
Yeah. It's for you.

362
00:21:13,560 --> 00:21:14,800
It's a doctor.

363
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,720
Hi, Doctor. You're never gonna believe it.

364
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,520
Are you at the hotel where your mum works?

365
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:22,480
- Yeah. - Can you let us in?

366
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:24,440
Frankie, where are you?

367
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:26,600
Don't keep me waiting.

368
00:22:23,480 --> 00:22:25,240
Kevin! Kevin!

369
00:22:25,680 --> 00:22:27,080
Oh, my God!

370
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:34,520
Get it off me! Help!

371
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:39,000
Hi! Yaz's mum.

372
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:40,960
Najia.

373
00:22:41,240 --> 00:22:42,480
Yaz, they can't be here.

374
00:22:42,560 --> 00:22:45,440
Najia, you made a very awesome human. Tell me what's going on.

375
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:48,800
- Was that gunshots? - Come on.

376
00:22:49,160 --> 00:22:50,160
Kevin!

377
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:53,320
Is it dead?

378
00:22:54,840 --> 00:22:56,160
Kevin?

379
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:09,200
Oh, God!

380
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:12,280
Oh, my God!

381
00:23:12,920 --> 00:23:13,960
He got Kevin.

382
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:16,840
I have no more Kevin.

383
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:19,760
I'm compromised.

384
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:27,040
Oh, no, my cell!

385
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:35,640
Help me! Help me!

386
00:23:46,680 --> 00:23:48,440
You are joking!

387
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
- No way. - Oh, no.

388
00:23:52,600 --> 00:23:53,840
Crisis investigators.

389
00:23:53,880 --> 00:23:56,040
You just ran really quickly out of a room looking really scared.

390
00:23:56,080 --> 00:23:57,400
Tell me exactly what's going on,

391
00:23:57,480 --> 00:23:59,520
omitting no detail, no matter how strange.

392
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:01,760
A giant spider just smashed through my bathtub

393
00:24:01,840 --> 00:24:03,680
and took out my bodyguard Kevin.

394
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:06,320
Right.

395
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:07,960
Very succinct summary. Well done.

396
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:10,920
You just wait here with my people.

397
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:13,600
Spider smashed through a bath.

398
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:16,400
Right.

399
00:24:25,120 --> 00:24:26,120
Hey.

400
00:24:26,520 --> 00:24:27,520
Hey.

401
00:24:29,080 --> 00:24:30,200
You're that bloke.

402
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:32,320
Yeah.

403
00:24:33,160 --> 00:24:34,560
All right.

404
00:24:44,480 --> 00:24:46,240
The room next door is covered in cobwebs.

405
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:48,840
- Top to bottom. - Just like your neighbour's house.

406
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:50,440
- What? - Big spider problem

407
00:24:50,520 --> 00:24:53,040
in this city right now, Yaz. Glad to be home?

408
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:23,160
Hi.

409
00:25:25,880 --> 00:25:27,320
Looking for a Kevin.

410
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:30,680
- See it? - Really close.

411
00:25:34,280 --> 00:25:36,080
Did you see it? Did you find him?

412
00:25:36,240 --> 00:25:37,880
Where's Kevin?

413
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:40,800
We need to move out of this area, quick. It's too dark, too deserted.

414
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:41,880
Spiders love that.

415
00:25:41,960 --> 00:25:43,560
We need to go somewhere bright and busy.

416
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:47,160
No, no, no. No, we need to get as far away from that thing as possible.

417
00:25:47,240 --> 00:25:48,880
- I'm with him. - I know the way out.

418
00:25:49,160 --> 00:25:51,480
- Everyone, follow Yaz's mum. - It's Najia.

419
00:25:51,880 --> 00:25:53,520
Wait!

420
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:55,640
- Who are you people? - Word of advice, mate.

421
00:25:55,720 --> 00:25:57,000
Run now, ask questions later.

422
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:00,040
Oh, that's bad.

423
00:26:00,400 --> 00:26:01,400
Oh.

424
00:26:04,760 --> 00:26:06,120
We just came in that way.

425
00:26:06,200 --> 00:26:07,800
How have they done so many webs so fast?

426
00:26:07,880 --> 00:26:09,680
Never mind that, let's just get through 'em.

427
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:11,560
No, this can't be happening.

428
00:26:11,720 --> 00:26:15,200
This is a protest. One of those eco-protests, huh?

429
00:26:15,320 --> 00:26:17,320
This isn't spiders. Spiders can't do that.

430
00:26:17,400 --> 00:26:20,240
These spiders can. Those aren't normal cobwebs.

431
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:23,280
They know we're here, and they're trying to seal us in.

432
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:26,400
They're trying to make the whole hotel their web.

433
00:26:26,600 --> 00:26:28,640
And we're the flies.

434
00:26:28,720 --> 00:26:29,760
We're not leaving.

435
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:33,880
We have to find out why they're here and stop them getting any further.

436
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:36,360
We need to find a safe haven. Yaz's mum?

437
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:37,680
Kitchen?

438
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:39,160
Lead on.

439
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:42,360
Good. This will do.

440
00:26:42,440 --> 00:26:45,840
Okay, thinking. Need to be quick. Spiders move in fast.

441
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:48,920
Why is this hotel the epicentre of spider activity?

442
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:50,520
Wait!

443
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:54,160
Nobody talk until you tell me what you're all doing here!

444
00:26:54,680 --> 00:26:56,800
"Spiders"?

445
00:26:57,680 --> 00:26:58,920
- Plural? - Very plural.

446
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:00,440
Sorry, I don't know who you are.

447
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:01,720
Oh! Really?

448
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:04,440
Because you must be the only person on the planet that doesn't.

449
00:27:04,520 --> 00:27:06,320
Are you Ed Sheeran? Is he Ed Sheeran?

450
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:08,760
Everyone talks about Ed Sheeran round about now, don't they?

451
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:12,640
I am not Ed Sheeran, I am Jack Robertson and this is my hotel.

452
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:17,080
Just one hotel in an incredibly successful chain of hotels,

453
00:27:17,240 --> 00:27:20,400
which is just one small part of my business portfolio

454
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:22,640
as featured in Fortune Global 500.

455
00:27:22,720 --> 00:27:24,200
Does that ring a bell?

456
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:26,240
Should I look impressed right now? Is that impressive?

457
00:27:26,280 --> 00:27:28,200
He's running for President in 2020.

458
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:31,320
- Ed Sheeran? - No, him. Robertson. Aren't you?

459
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:33,360
Well, I haven't declared my intentions yet.

460
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:35,800
But, look, we're talking about spiders.

461
00:27:35,920 --> 00:27:38,080
See? Typical politician, avoiding the question.

462
00:27:38,160 --> 00:27:40,160
I am not a politician, I'm a businessman

463
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:41,560
and I know how to run things.

464
00:27:41,640 --> 00:27:44,560
I've heard you're only running because you've hated Trump for decades.

465
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:47,200
Please don't mention that name.

466
00:27:47,320 --> 00:27:51,720
Look, I was just attacked by a spider the size of a bathtub

467
00:27:51,800 --> 00:27:55,800
- and it's all her fault! - I told you, I know nothing about this.

468
00:27:55,880 --> 00:27:58,040
Mum, don't even talk to him, he fired you.

469
00:27:58,120 --> 00:27:59,280
What? He didn't!

470
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:02,080
You didn't! You can't be President if you fire Yaz's mum.

471
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:03,560
- Najia. - I hate spiders.

472
00:28:03,800 --> 00:28:05,880
- Phobia-hate. - Oh, man. Me too.

473
00:28:06,160 --> 00:28:08,040
And there's loads of them knocking about right now.

474
00:28:08,080 --> 00:28:10,480
I will not have them in my hotel.

475
00:28:10,560 --> 00:28:12,160
I mean, you're running for President,

476
00:28:12,280 --> 00:28:14,880
it could be espionage, you know, targeted directly...

477
00:28:15,040 --> 00:28:16,720
At you by Russians!

478
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:19,600
That's possible. There are a lot of people that would like to see me dead.

479
00:28:19,640 --> 00:28:21,000
- Funny that. - See?

480
00:28:21,080 --> 00:28:22,760
They don't need giant spiders,

481
00:28:22,840 --> 00:28:24,600
they'd just pop a tiny poisonous one on your pillow.

482
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:27,840
You've really got to stop saying stuff like that.

483
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:30,160
- So, what do we do? - Why are you asking her?

484
00:28:30,280 --> 00:28:32,360
- 'Cause she's in charge, bro. - Says who?

485
00:28:32,440 --> 00:28:33,560
Says us!

486
00:28:33,680 --> 00:28:35,080
How do you all know each other?

487
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:36,920
Mum, can you shut up a second?

488
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:38,440
Right, we need two things.

489
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:40,680
Plans of the hotel and a captive spider.

490
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:46,880
I'm not getting near the spider.

491
00:28:50,600 --> 00:28:52,760
I really don't like this.

492
00:28:52,840 --> 00:28:54,640
I don't want to do this.

493
00:28:54,720 --> 00:28:57,920
I can't stand it. Come on, Graham, deep breaths now.

494
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:06,760
Sorry, mate, don't mind me.

495
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:11,520
No, no, go on. You carry on.

496
00:29:11,880 --> 00:29:13,800
Stay away. Go on, stay away.

497
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:15,280
Nothin' to see here.

498
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:16,680
I'm warnin' ya.

499
00:29:17,840 --> 00:29:18,920
Go on, get away!

500
00:29:19,320 --> 00:29:21,760
Ooh, I've got ya!

501
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:23,040
You took your time!

502
00:29:23,200 --> 00:29:26,640
- One, two, three, lift it! - Ooh!

503
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:28,480
- Graham! - What?

504
00:29:28,560 --> 00:29:30,120
- Behind you! - All right! Leg it!

505
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:32,240
Faster, come on!

506
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:38,600
Now, hotel plans. Let's see.

507
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:43,640
That can't be accurate, it doesn't even have my panic room marked on it.

508
00:29:45,480 --> 00:29:47,960
Any issues with spiders before today, Najia,

509
00:29:48,040 --> 00:29:50,240
- here or at home? - My home?

510
00:29:50,320 --> 00:29:51,920
One of your neighbours had a spider problem.

511
00:29:51,960 --> 00:29:53,920
The link between both places is you.

512
00:29:54,080 --> 00:29:58,840
I knew it, and I'm going to litigate you until your last breath, Nah-dia.

513
00:29:59,040 --> 00:30:01,160
It's Najia, and I've done nothing.

514
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:04,560
- Are you sure? - Sorry, but who are you?

515
00:30:04,640 --> 00:30:07,120
How do you know my daughter? Why haven't I met you before?

516
00:30:07,200 --> 00:30:08,200
Oh, not now.

517
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:10,640
Oh, yes, now. It's not a difficult question.

518
00:30:10,720 --> 00:30:13,520
- It is a bit of a long answer. - Well, I've got time.

519
00:30:13,880 --> 00:30:16,280
But I haven't. Not right now.

520
00:30:18,440 --> 00:30:20,000
Are you two seeing each other?

521
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:24,400
I don't think so. Are we?

522
00:30:25,560 --> 00:30:27,600
- We're friends! - Mmm.

523
00:30:27,680 --> 00:30:30,720
I owe the Doctor my life, quite a few times over.

524
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:32,200
What is that even supposed to mean?

525
00:30:32,280 --> 00:30:34,960
Please, can we not have this conversation now?

526
00:30:35,080 --> 00:30:36,240
And not in front of him.

527
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:37,880
Oh, I'm enjoying this.

528
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,400
